This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints for the parameters $\beta$, $\lambda$, $\mu$, and $f$, which enable the existence of a localized ground state solution for the given equation. Additionally, this paper examines the normalizable ground state solution for a generalized Brézis-Nirenberg type problem of the biharmonic equation when the parameter $\lambda$ varies within a certain range. No ground state solution exists for the given problem when $\lambda$ exceeds a certain critical value. This existence/nonexistence result for the ground state solution sheds light on the topological mountain pass characterization of the positive solution space for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation. Furthermore, this paper establishes the $C^1$-norm of the manifold consisting of the normalized ground state solutions for the given equation when the parameter $\beta$ lies within a specific range. This characterization is derived from an analysis of the spectrum of the extended operator associated with the biharmonic Choquard type equation, as well as the application of the Liusternik-Schnirelmann theory. This study extends and improves earlier research by R. A. Gooding, M. R. R. R. R. Gooding, and M. R. R. R. R. Gooding, whose results were limited to the case where $\beta=0$. This work also resolves an open problem proposed by J. Yang, X. Yang, and J. L. Yang. This work was initially presented as part of the Ph.D. research of the first-named author under the supervision of T. Yang at Zhejiang University. This work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China Grant 12131013, the Zhejiang University Qian-Ji Wei

The proliferation of computing technology and wearable devices like smartphones and wristbands allows users to easily access health data including activity levels, sleep patterns, and sports history. Successful smart healthcare relies on training machine learning models using a large quantity of user data, but several key challenges arise. Primarily, user data exists in disparate segments, complicating data aggregation without breaching privacy protocols. Secondly, models trained on cloud platforms struggle to accommodate personalization requirements. This paper introduces FedHealth, a pioneering federated transfer learning framework for wearable healthcare designed to address these difficulties. FedHealth aggregates data via federated learning and employs transfer learning to develop personalized models. It succeeds in providing accurate, personalized healthcare without compromising security and data confidentiality. Experimentation reveals that FedHealth yields 5.3% greater accuracy in wearable activity recognition when compared to conventional approaches. Furthermore, FedHealth demonstrates versatility, extending to other healthcare domains like sleep quality and sports. Its performance in these areas surpasses baseline models by 12.9% and 11.9% respectively. The code for this research is available on an open-source platform. With its enhanced performance and ease of use, FedHealth offers a substantial framework for various wearable healthcare applications. It has the capability to handle various healthcare domains and could be a valuable tool for healthcare companies interested in federated learning solutions. To facilitate user access, the framework has a user-friendly website where potential users can obtain code, data, and models for evaluation. This approach has the potential to contribute significantly to the advancement of healthcare technology. To facilitate user access, the framework has a user-friendly website where potential users can obtain code, data